# interplay_limits_iosurface_ring: The IOSurface host device (map and isolate only: QEMU creates it only with a memory map) at its limits. 256 surfaces, one on each of 256 descriptor pages (v2, the last slot of each: IDs 15, 31, ... 4095, all over one guest page), and ID 8191 (the largest, on descriptor page 511, over a page of its own). One doorbell fills the whole 1024-entry ring: 257 maps, a map of 8192 (BadArgument), and 766 tests of 8191, which write their pattern into its page; 1024 interrupts, the read counter at 1024. Task 1 renders clears through mapper textures (type 11) into surfaces 15 and 8191, 8191 is presented (opcode 8), rendered into again while the display holds it. A second full lap of the ring unmaps all 256: 15 is Busy, because task 1's mapper texture still holds it (a retain count other than 1; under isolation too), and the other 255 unmap; 8191 is Busy (the display and the texture hold it); 15 is tested (still mapped: its pattern lands in the shared page) and mapped again (already mapped: success); the read counter ends at 2048.
